Data Preprocessing:

1. Program Purpose
The program connects:
 Data Quality
 Data Cleaning
 Data Transformation
 Data Reduction
 Proximity Measures and Machine Learning

The program reads the ecommerce email marketing dataset, checks problems, fixes them, demonstrates distance/proximity measures, trains ML models, and saves CSV reports.

2. Importing Libraries
import os
import re
import time
import warnings
from pathlib import Path

import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
Meaning
These are basic utility libraries.

os checks whether file paths exist.
time measures model training time.
Path creates output folders.
numpy handles numerical operations.
pandas handles the dataset.

The ML libraries include models, preprocessing tools, pipelines, train-test split, and performance metrics.

3. File Path Configuration
DATA_PATH = r"D:\dataset\bits\ecommerce_email_marketing_dataset.csv"
OUTPUT_FOLDER = r"D:\dataset\bits"
What it does
This tells Python where the dataset is stored and where the output files should be saved.

Example:

D:\dataset\bits\ecommerce_email_marketing_dataset.csv
If the file is not found in Windows, the script uses /mnt/data/... as a fallback for testing.

4. Target Column
TARGET_COLUMN = "purchased"
Meaning
The model is trying to predict whether a customer purchased or not.

Example:

customer	purchased
C001	Yes
C002	No
This is a classification problem.

Important Functions Used in the Code
5. add_report() Function
def add_report(theory_week, problem, columns, before, after, action_taken):
What this function does
This function records every preprocessing action in a validation report.

It stores:
Problem	Missing values, duplicate rows, outliers, etc.
Columns Checked	Which columns were checked
Before	Problem count before fixing
After	Problem count after fixing
Action Taken	What was done

Example:
Problem: Duplicate records
Before: 143
After: 0
Action Taken: Removed duplicate rows
This is later saved as week7_preprocessing_validation_report.csv.

6. clean_column_names() Function
def clean_column_names(dataframe):
What it does
This function standardizes column names.

It performs:
.str.strip()
.str.lower()
.str.replace(" ", "_")
.str.replace(r"[^a-z0-9_]", "")

Example
Before:
Customer ID
Open Rate
Last Purchase Days

After:
customer_id
open_rate
last_purchase_days

Why this is important
Without this, Python code becomes difficult because Open Rate, openrate, and OpenRate may be treated differently.

7. find_existing_columns() Function
def find_existing_columns(dataframe, possible_names):
What it does
This function checks which column names exist in the dataset.

Example:

find_existing_columns(df, ["openrate", "open_rate", "email_open_rate"])
If the dataset contains open_rate, it returns:
["open_rate"]

Why this is useful
Different datasets may use different column names. This makes the program flexible.

8. make_onehot_encoder() Function
def make_onehot_encoder():
What it does
This creates a OneHotEncoder.

One-hot encoding converts categorical values into numeric columns.

Example:

device_type
Mobile
Desktop
Becomes:

device_type_Mobile	device_type_Desktop
1	0
0	1
The function also handles different scikit-learn versions. Some versions use sparse_output=False, older versions use sparse=False.

Main Program Flow
9. Load Dataset
df = pd.read_csv(DATA_PATH)
What it does
Reads the CSV file into a pandas DataFrame.

It also prints:

Original shape
Original columns
Example:

Original shape: (10000, 20)
That means 10,000 rows and 20 columns.

10. Standardize Column Names
df = clean_column_names(df)
Example
Before:

Email Provider
Campaign Type
After:

email_provider
campaign_type
Then the code adds this action to the validation report using add_report().

11. Handle Empty Strings and Text Missing Values
df = df.replace(r"^\s*$", np.nan, regex=True)
df = df.replace(["Unknown", "NA", "null", "None"], np.nan)
What it does
Converts fake missing values into real missing values.

Example:

income
50000
Unknown
70000
Becomes:

income
50000
NaN
NaN
70000
This allows proper imputation later.

12. Remove Duplicate Rows
df = df.drop_duplicates().reset_index(drop=True)
What it does
Removes repeated rows.

Example:

customer_id	email
C001	a@gmail.com
C001	a@gmail.com
Only one row remains.

Duplicates can bias the model because the same customer is counted more than once.

13. Remove Extra Spaces
df[col] = df[col].astype(str).str.strip()
What it does
Removes leading and trailing spaces from text columns.

Example:

" Hyderabad "
becomes:

"Hyderabad"
Without this, Python treats "Hyderabad" and " Hyderabad " as different cities.

14. Standardize Mixed Letter Cases
df[col] = df[col].str.lower()
or

df[col] = df[col].str.title()
Example
Email provider:

GMAIL, Gmail, gmail
becomes:

gmail
Campaign type or city:

hyderabad
becomes:

Hyderabad
This reduces fake categories.

15. Clean Gender Values
def clean_gender(value):
What it does
Maps multiple gender formats into standard categories.

Example:

m, male, Male, MAN
becomes:

Male
f, female, Female, WOMAN
becomes:

Female
Unknown values become NaN.

16. Validate Email Format
email_regex = r"^[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@[A-Za-z0-9.-]+\.[A-Za-z]{2,}$"
What it does
Checks whether emails are valid.

Example:

email	is_valid_email	email_domain
raj@gmail.com	1	gmail.com
raj@gmail	0	invalid_email
The code creates two new columns:

is_valid_email
email_domain
This converts messy email data into useful features.

17. Convert Numeric Columns
df[col] = pd.to_numeric(df[col], errors="coerce")
What it does
Converts numeric-looking columns into actual numbers.

Example:

income
50000
Unknown
70000
After conversion:

income
50000
NaN
70000
Invalid text becomes NaN.

18. Fix Invalid Numeric Values
The code checks:

age < 0 or age > 100
open_rate < 0 or open_rate > 1
income < 0
total_spent < 0
Example
age
35
-5
150
After cleaning:

age
35
NaN
NaN
These invalid values are later filled using imputation.

19. Convert Date Columns
df[col] = pd.to_datetime(df[col], errors="coerce", dayfirst=True)
What it does
Converts date text into date format.

Then it creates:

year
month
dayofweek
days_since_date
Example:

signup_date = 15-01-2023
becomes:

signup_date_year = 2023
signup_date_month = 1
signup_date_dayofweek = 6
days_since_signup_date = 900
Machine learning cannot directly understand dates, but it can use numerical features extracted from dates.

20. Clean Email Subject Text
.str.lower()
.str.replace(r"[^a-z0-9\s]", "")
What it does
Removes symbols and punctuation from email subject lines.

Example:
🔥 Huge SALE!!! 70% OFF ###

becomes:
huge sale 70 off

Then it creates:
email_subject_length
email_subject_word_count
These become numerical text features.

21. Convert Boolean Text to Numbers
yes -> 1
no -> 0
true -> 1
false -> 0
Example
subscribed
Yes
No
Yes
becomes:

subscribed
1
0
1
ML models require numerical input.

22. Treat Outliers Using IQR
q1 = df[col].quantile(0.25)
q3 = df[col].quantile(0.75)
iqr = q3 - q1
lower = q1 - 1.5 * iqr
upper = q3 + 1.5 * iqr
df[col] = df[col].clip(lower=lower, upper=upper)
What it does
Caps extremely high or low values.

Example:

income
45000
52000
61000
9000000
The value 9000000 may be capped to the calculated upper limit.

This prevents extreme values from dominating the model.

23. Create Log-Transformed Features
df[f"{col}_log1p"] = np.log1p(df[col])
What it does
Creates a new transformed version of skewed columns like income or total spending.

Example:

income = 1000000
After log transformation:

income_log1p = log(1000001)
This reduces skewness and makes values less extreme.

24. Drop High-Cardinality and Irrelevant Columns
customer_id
customer_name
email
are dropped.

Why?
customer_id is only an identifier.
email is usually unique for every row.
These columns do not generalize well for ML.

However, useful information like email_domain and is_valid_email is kept.

25. Drop Leakage Columns
purchased_amount
is dropped.

Why?
If we are predicting whether a customer purchased, the purchase amount may directly reveal the answer.

Example:

purchased	purchased_amount
Yes	2500
No	0
This would make the model unrealistically accurate.

26. Remove Highly Correlated Features
corr_matrix = numeric_df_for_corr.corr().abs()
What it does
Finds features with correlation greater than 0.95.

Example:

open_rate and click_rate correlation = 0.98
One of them may be removed because both provide almost the same information.

This is data reduction.

27. Clean Target Variable
yes -> 1
no -> 0
The target column purchased is converted to numeric.

Rows with missing target values are removed because supervised ML needs a known answer.

28. Check Class Imbalance
df[TARGET_COLUMN].value_counts(normalize=True)
Example
0 = 85%
1 = 15%
This means most customers did not purchase.

The code handles this by using:

stratify=y
class_weight="balanced"
This makes training more fair.

29. Separate Features and Target
X = df.drop(columns=[TARGET_COLUMN])
y = df[TARGET_COLUMN]
Meaning
X contains input variables:

age, income, city, device_type, open_rate
y contains the answer:

purchased
Then the code separates numeric and categorical columns.

30. Build the Preprocessing Pipeline
Numeric Pipeline
numeric_transformer = Pipeline([
    ("imputer", SimpleImputer(strategy="median")),
    ("scaler", StandardScaler())
])
It does two things:

Missing numeric values are filled with median.

Example:

income = NaN
becomes:

income = median income
Then scaling is applied.

Example:

income = 100000
age = 35
open_rate = 0.25
Scaling makes them comparable.

Categorical Pipeline
categorical_transformer = Pipeline([
    ("imputer", SimpleImputer(strategy="most_frequent")),
    ("encoder", OneHotEncoder())
])
It fills missing categorical values with the most common value and converts categories into numeric columns.

Example:

device_type = Mobile
becomes:

device_type_Mobile = 1
device_type_Desktop = 0
The ColumnTransformer applies numeric treatment to numeric columns and categorical treatment to categorical columns.

31. Week 7 Proximity Measures Demo
The code chooses numeric features such as:

age
income
open_rate
click_rate
It compares two customers using:

Euclidean distance
Manhattan distance
Cosine similarity
Why scaling matters
Before scaling:

income = 120000
age = 35
open_rate = 0.25
Income dominates the distance calculation because it has very large values.

After scaling:

income, age, open_rate
come to comparable scale.

This is very important for KNN because KNN depends on distance.

32. Train-Test Split
train_test_split(
    X,
    y,
    test_size=0.20,
    random_state=42,
    stratify=y
)
What it does
Splits data into:

80% training data
20% testing data
stratify=y keeps the same purchased/not-purchased ratio in both train and test data.

33. Define Machine Learning Models
The code uses five models:

Logistic Regression
Decision Tree
Random Forest
Gradient Boosting
K-Nearest Neighbors
At least four were required, and the code uses five.

34. Train, Time, and Evaluate Models
For every model:

pipeline = Pipeline([
    ("preprocessing", preprocessor),
    ("model", model)
])
Meaning
Each model first gets the same preprocessing treatment, then it is trained.

Training time is measured using:

start_time = time.perf_counter()
pipeline.fit(X_train, y_train)
end_time = time.perf_counter()
Accuracy is calculated using:

accuracy_score(y_test, y_pred)
The program also calculates:

Precision
Recall
F1 Score
ROC AUC
True Positive
True Negative
False Positive
False Negative
This gives a more complete performance comparison than accuracy alone.

35. Save Output Files
The program saves four output files:

week7_preprocessing_validation_report.csv
week7_model_results_after_preprocessing.csv
week7_proximity_measures_demo.csv
week7_cleaned_ecommerce_dataset.csv

Learning Objectives:
 What problems existed
 What preprocessing fixed them
 How proximity measures changed after scaling
 Which model performed best
 How much time each model took

